-- title:
--   Theorem 5.5 — tree-unfolding characterisation
-- statement:
--   Fix $b\in\mathbb N$. A family $(T_n)_{n\ge0}$ of rooted, naturally labelled, ordered trees is generated by a deterministic decreasing-argument recursive specification with at most $b$ immediate calls per invocation if and only if the family is recursively consistent and every vertex has at most $b$ children.
--
--   Recursive consistency means that $T_n$ has root label $n$, labels strictly decrease along every parent–child edge, and every subtree rooted at label $k$ is $T_k$ as a labelled ordered tree.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: isomorphism as a labelled ordered tree → equality of values of an inductive finite labelled ordered-tree type; reason: the inductive representation has no vertex identities beyond labels and ordered tree structure, so equality is precisely the stated isomorphism
--   - encoding: rooted integer-labelled trees indexed by nonnegative inputs → trees with natural-number labels; reason: every label is an input to the decreasing recursion and hence is nonnegative
-- source:
--   Swain, Recursive Call-Count Sequences: Structural Realisability, Periodic Branching, and Partial-Observation Reconstruction, STOC 2027 submission (manuscript sha256 79135884…), Theorem 5.5, p.10

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_CallCountPOPBR_Supplement_DecSpec_unfold
import Definitions.Def_CallCountPOPBR_Supplement_RecConsistent

set_option autoImplicit false

namespace CallCountPOPBR.Supplement

/-- **Theorem 5.5 (Tree-unfolding characterisation).** Swain, *Recursive Call-Count Sequences: Structural Realisability, Periodic Branching, and
Partial-Observation Reconstruction*, STOC 2027 submission (manuscript sha256 79135884…), Theorem 5.5, p.10: "A family (T_n) is
generated by a deterministic decreasing-argument recursive specification with at most b immediate
calls per invocation if and only if it is recursively consistent and each vertex has at most b
children."

Generated: `T n = s.unfold n` for every `n`, for some decreasing-argument specification `s` making
at most `b` calls at each input. "Each vertex has at most `b` children" ranges over every vertex of
every `T_n`. -/
theorem theorem_5_5 (b : ℕ) (T : ℕ → LTree) :
    (∃ s : DecSpec, (∀ n, (s.calls n).length ≤ b) ∧ ∀ n, T n = s.unfold n) ↔
      (RecConsistent T ∧ ∀ n t, LTree.Sub t (T n) → t.children.length ≤ b) := by sorry

end CallCountPOPBR.Supplement
